WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.250
This is Allen Company task training for haul truck

00:07.250 --> 00:10.188
drivers, and for anyone who drives a tri-axle road

00:10.188 --> 00:13.463
truck on mine property. Part 48 says before you're

00:13.463 --> 00:16.325
assigned a task, you learn the safe work procedures

00:16.325 --> 00:19.300
for that task, the standards that apply to it,

00:19.300 --> 00:21.762
and the hazards you'll face. That's what this lesson

00:21.762 --> 00:22.637
covers.

00:23.748 --> 00:26.598
At our quarries you'll see two kinds of trucks.

00:26.598 --> 00:29.398
Off-road haul trucks move shot rock from the face

00:29.398 --> 00:31.810
to the crusher. At every Allen site,

00:31.810 --> 00:34.310
tri-axle trucks, with a steer axle,

00:34.310 --> 00:37.011
a lift or pusher axle and a tandem,

00:37.011 --> 00:39.623
also haul stone inside the pit, so they're mine

00:39.623 --> 00:42.323
haulage equipment too. Both are self-propelled

00:42.323 --> 00:45.261
mobile equipment under MSHA, so the same rules

00:45.261 --> 00:47.898
apply to both while they're on mine property.

00:51.227 --> 00:53.164
Before you put the truck into service,

00:53.164 --> 00:58.514
every shift, you examine it. Under 56.14100,

00:58.514 --> 01:01.452
self-propelled equipment is inspected by the operator

01:01.452 --> 01:04.776
before use on that shift. Walk all the way around.

01:04.776 --> 01:08.389
Tires and lug nuts. Air lines and brake components.

01:08.389 --> 01:11.339
Lights, horn and backup alarm. Mirrors,

01:11.339 --> 01:14.352
windows and wipers. Steps and handholds.

01:14.352 --> 01:17.389
The hoist, the body and the tailgate latch.

01:17.389 --> 01:19.901
Look underneath for fresh leaks. Then check the

01:19.901 --> 01:23.839
cab: seat belt, gauges, the fire extinguisher,

01:23.839 --> 01:25.802
and a brake test before you move.

01:26.920 --> 01:29.270
If you find a defect that affects safety,

01:29.270 --> 01:31.832
the truck doesn't run until it's fixed.

01:31.832 --> 01:34.482
Tag it out of service and tell your supervisor.

01:34.482 --> 01:37.532
Other defects get reported and corrected in a reasonable

01:37.532 --> 01:40.132
time, and the inspection is recorded.

01:40.132 --> 01:43.420
At Allen, record it on the Quarry Equipment Pre-Operational

01:43.420 --> 01:47.470
Inspection, every time, even if it's "no defects,"

01:47.470 --> 01:49.932
and turn it in at the scale house or office,

01:49.932 --> 01:51.108
or through the app.

01:52.246 --> 01:54.709
Service brakes must stop and hold the truck with

01:54.709 --> 01:57.159
its typical load on the grades it travels,

01:57.159 --> 01:59.371
and the parking brake must hold it on the grades

01:59.371 --> 02:02.309
where you park. Seat belts are provided and worn

02:02.309 --> 02:05.384
in haulage trucks. Belt on before the truck moves,

02:05.384 --> 02:06.334
every time.

02:09.668 --> 02:12.280
A loaded tri-axle sits higher than you think.

02:12.280 --> 02:14.680
From the driver's seat, whole zones around the

02:14.680 --> 02:17.518
truck disappear: right along the front bumper,

02:17.518 --> 02:20.380
down the passenger side, and a long stretch behind

02:20.380 --> 02:22.668
the tailgate. If you're standing in one of these

02:22.668 --> 02:25.655
zones, the driver cannot see you. So before you

02:25.655 --> 02:28.068
approach, make contact with the driver,

02:28.068 --> 02:30.905
and wait for an answer back. No acknowledgment,

02:30.905 --> 02:32.081
no approach.

02:33.206 --> 02:35.694
On an off-road haul truck, the blind zones are

02:35.694 --> 02:38.706
much bigger. A whole pickup can sit in the right-front

02:38.706 --> 02:41.444
blind area and the driver will never see it.

02:41.444 --> 02:44.356
That's why, at Allen, a radio call to the haul

02:44.356 --> 02:47.469
truck is required before you approach it in a pickup.

02:47.469 --> 02:49.319
No answer, no approach.

02:50.445 --> 02:52.857
When you're the driver, it works the other way.

02:52.857 --> 02:55.594
Before you move, make sure everyone is clear,

02:55.594 --> 02:58.419
and sound a warning if anyone could be exposed.

02:58.419 --> 03:01.070
Keep the horn and backup alarm working;

03:01.070 --> 03:03.757
if the alarm quits, report it before you back up

03:03.757 --> 03:06.019
again. If you lose sight of someone who was near

03:06.019 --> 03:08.582
your truck, stop. Don't move until you know where

03:08.582 --> 03:09.394
they are.

03:12.714 --> 03:15.451
Each mine has traffic rules: speed limits,

03:15.451 --> 03:19.251
right-of-way, signs and signals. At all our sites,

03:19.251 --> 03:21.589
haul trucks have the right of way, and traffic

03:21.589 --> 03:24.526
is two-way and keeps to the right. The speed limit

03:24.526 --> 03:27.889
is 15 miles per hour on site; at Boonesboro it's

03:27.889 --> 03:31.076
15 on the surface and 25 underground.

03:31.076 --> 03:33.639
Keep your truck under control at all times.

03:33.639 --> 03:35.976
Drive at a speed that lets you stop in the distance

03:35.976 --> 03:38.814
you can see, and slow down for dust,

03:38.814 --> 03:41.014
rain, ice and darkness.

03:42.132 --> 03:44.769
Where a road has a drop-off big enough to overturn

03:44.769 --> 03:47.507
a truck, there's a berm or guardrail.

03:47.507 --> 03:49.957
The berm has to be at least mid-axle height of

03:49.957 --> 03:53.144
the largest equipment that usually uses that road.

03:53.144 --> 03:56.294
A berm is a reminder, not a wall; it won't stop

03:56.294 --> 03:58.957
a loaded truck. If you see a berm that's been knocked

03:58.957 --> 04:01.969
down or washed out, report it right away.

04:03.102 --> 04:05.927
At dump points with a drop-off, there's a berm,

04:05.927 --> 04:08.827
bumper block or similar restraint to stop you.

04:08.827 --> 04:11.514
Back up straight and slow, use the restraint as

04:11.514 --> 04:14.539
a guide, not a stop, and dump short if the edge

04:14.539 --> 04:18.414
looks cracked or soft. Never rely on the edge itself.

04:19.536 --> 04:22.449
Never dump or park near overhead power lines,

04:22.449 --> 04:24.911
never travel with the bed raised, and don't tarp

04:24.911 --> 04:27.136
under a line. When you raise the bed,

04:27.136 --> 04:29.711
look up first for power lines and structures.

04:29.711 --> 04:33.024
Dump on level, firm ground; a raised bed on a slope

04:33.024 --> 04:35.786
can tip the whole truck. Bring the body all the

04:35.786 --> 04:37.736
way down before you pull away.

04:41.050 --> 04:43.575
Don't leave the truck unattended unless the controls

04:43.575 --> 04:46.213
are in park and the parking brake is set.

04:46.213 --> 04:48.825
On a grade, chock the wheels or turn them into

04:48.825 --> 04:51.563
a bank. That rule is on the books because trucks

04:51.563 --> 04:53.763
have rolled away and killed people.

04:54.892 --> 04:57.592
Get on and off facing the truck, with three points

04:57.592 --> 05:00.955
of contact. Don't jump. Most truck-driver injuries

05:00.955 --> 05:03.592
at quarries aren't wrecks; they're slips and falls

05:03.592 --> 05:05.130
getting in and out.

05:06.262 --> 05:10.375
Nobody rides on steps, fenders, beds or loads.

05:10.375 --> 05:13.212
Passengers ride only in a seat built for them,

05:13.212 --> 05:14.450
with a seat belt.

05:17.781 --> 05:20.493
Keep windows closed and the cab pressurized where

05:20.493 --> 05:23.456
it's equipped, to keep dust out. Haul trucks are

05:23.456 --> 05:26.380
loud; if your exposure needs it, wear the hearing

05:26.380 --> 05:28.918
protection you're issued. And if you're tired or

05:28.918 --> 05:31.430
taking medication that could affect your driving,

05:31.430 --> 05:33.680
tell your supervisor before you start.

05:33.680 --> 05:35.268
That's what Allen expects.

05:38.586 --> 05:41.936
Recap. Examine your truck every shift and tag out

05:41.936 --> 05:45.399
safety defects. Belt on. Make contact and get an

05:45.399 --> 05:47.948
answer back before you approach any truck,

05:47.948 --> 05:51.448
and as a driver, don't move until everyone is clear.

05:51.448 --> 05:54.311
Follow the traffic rules and keep it under control.

05:54.311 --> 05:57.448
Respect berms and dump edges. Park it so it can't

05:57.448 --> 05:58.173
move.

05:59.292 --> 06:01.667
If you're ever unsure, stop and ask.

06:01.667 --> 06:04.255
Your supervisor and the Safety Manager would rather

06:04.255 --> 06:07.117
answer a question than investigate an accident.

06:08.238 --> 06:11.401
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

06:11.401 --> 06:12.901
override this video.
